data.content:
Here's the full migration analysis and replacement.

## Problems in .NET 8

| API | Issue |
|-----|-------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (`SYSLIB0006`) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9), unreliable even in .NET 8 |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Not cancellation-aware — can't be woken by a token |
| Race condition | Multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` lose signals — only one item is dequeued |

## Patterns Identified

1. **Cancellable work loop** → `CancellationToken` checked in loop
2. **Blocking call interruption** → `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle`
3. **Thread.Abort as forced stop** → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _newEvent = new(false);
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly object _lock = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var handles = new WaitHandle[] { _newEvent, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };

        while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // WaitAny returns index: 0 = new event, 1 = cancellation
            int index = WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);
            if (index == 1)
                break; // cancellation requested

            _newEvent.Reset();

            // Drain all queued items (fixes the original race condition)
            while (true)
            {
                string? item;
                lock (_lock)
                {
                    if (_queue.Count == 0)
                        break;
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
                }
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();           // signals the token's WaitHandle
        _thread?.Join(5000);     // wait for graceful exit
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* ... */ }
}
```

## Key Changes

| Before | After | Why |
|--------|-------|-----|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Cooperative cancellation — no `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Removed | Token's `WaitHandle` wakes the thread instead |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles)` | Wakes on **either** new work or cancellation |
| Single `Dequeue` | Drain loop | Fixes bug: multiple `Enqueue` calls before `Reset` lost items |
| No cleanup | `IDisposable` | `CancellationTokenSource` and `WaitHandle` must be disposed |

## Pitfalls to Watch

1. **`Handle()` must be fast or check the token** — cancellation only takes effect at the next `WaitAny`. If `Handle()` is long-running, pass `_cts.Token` into it and call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints.
2. **Don't forget `Dispose`** — leaking `CancellationTokenSource` causes timer/callback leaks.
3. **`Stop()` is not instant** — the thread finishes its current `Handle()` call before exiting. The 5-second `Join` timeout is your safety net; after that, the thread is orphaned (no way to force-kill it in .NET 8).
